ned}\n\\mid x - 6 \\mid + 2x + 3 &=  \\\\\n\\mid - 3 - 6 \\mid + 2(-3) + 3 &=  \\\\\n\\mid - 9 \\mid + 2(-3) + 3 &=  \\\\\n9 + 2(-3) + 3 &=  \\\\\n9 - 6 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054470 1074 "We substitute $x = -3$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-6|+2x+3 & = \\\\ |-3-6|+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ |-9|+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ 9+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ 9-6+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054471 1071 "Evaluate $\\begin{vmatrix}2 - x & + 1 + 5 & x\\end{vmatrix}$ for $x = 5$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054471 1071 "Evaluate $|2 - x| + 1 + 5|x|$ for $x = 5.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054471 1074 "We substitute $x = 5$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - x & + 1 + 5 & x\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - 5 & + 1 + 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\mid - 3 \\mid + 1 + 5(5) &=  \\\\\n3 + 1 + 25 &=  \\\\\n4 + 25 &=  \\\\\n&= 29\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054471 1074 "We substitute $x = 5$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-x|+1+5|x| & = \\\\ |2-5|+1+5|5| & = \\\\ |-3|+1+5(5) & = \\\\ 3+1+25 & = \\\\ 4+25 & = \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054576 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\mid -18a \\mid$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054576 1071 "Simplify the expression $|-18a|.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054576 1074 "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-a & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\mid 18a \\mid\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 18a \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\mid 18 \\cdot a \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}18 & \\cdot & a\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n18 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}a & &= \\\\ 18 & a\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054576 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|18a|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|18a| & = \\\\ |18\\cdot a| & = \\\\ |18|\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054577 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\left|-\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054577 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\left|-\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054577 1074 "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-a & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\frac{9}{5c} \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}9 &}{\\mid 5c\\end{vmatrix}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9}{\\mid 5c\\begin{vmatrix}} &= \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\cdot & c\\end{vmatrix}}|= \\\\ \\frac{9}{5 \\mid c\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186054577 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{9}{5c}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|9|}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5|\\cdot |c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{5|c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481347 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481392 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481436 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481358 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481394 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481409 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481362 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481366 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481397 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481441 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481354 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054923 1050 420.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186054936 1050 420.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055103 1071 "If $t = 0$, then $\\begin{vmatrix}t - 2 & -\\end{vmatrix}t + 3|=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186055103 1071 "If $t=0,$ then $|t-2|-|t+3|=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055103 1074 "We substitute $t=0$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|t-2|-|t+3| & = \\\\ |0-2|-|0+3| & = \\\\ |-2|-|3| & = \\\\ 2-3 & = \\\\ -1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055225 1071 "$|-\\frac{4z}{7}|=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055225 1071 "What is $\\mid - \\frac{4z}{7} \\mid$?" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186055225 1074 "Since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-\\frac{4z}{7}|=|\\frac{4z}{7}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055949 1064 "Recall that the absolute value of a number represents the distance of that number from the origin.\n\nLet's take a look at the number line below.\n\n![](images/e2/e20db43f722fc4db42fc845ab20a7eef2c06437cc1d2246e0aed47dc01107aba.png)\n\nSince $-3$ and $3$ are at the same distance from the origin, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & = & 3\\end{vmatrix} = 3\n$$\n\nIn general, for any real number $a$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-a & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify expressions containing absolute value. Let's see an example." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186055949 1064 "Recall that the absolute value of a number represents the distance of that number from the origin.\n\nLet's take a look at the number line below.\n\n![](images/e2/e20db43f722fc4db42fc845ab20a7eef2c06437cc1d2246e0aed47dc01107aba.png)\n\nSince $-3$ and $3$ are at the same distance from the origin, we have\n\n$$\n|-3| = |3| = 3.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any real number $a,$\n\n$$\n|-a| = |a|.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify expressions containing absolute value. Let's see an example." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186055996 1064 "The absolute value operation is **distributive with respect to multiplication.** This means that the absolute value of a product equals the product of the absolute values.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\mid (-6) \\cdot 2 \\mid\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this expression in two separate ways:\n\n- The first is to find the absolute value of the product. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}(-6) \\cdot 2 & &= & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second is to find the product of the absolute values. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}(-6) \\cdot 2 & &= & -6 & \\cdot & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a \\cdot b & = & a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value operation is also distributive with respect to division. In general,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right| = \\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}a &}{\\mid b\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186055996 1064 "The absolute value operation is **distributive with respect to multiplication.** This means that the absolute value of a product equals the product of the absolute values.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n|(-6) \\cdot 2|\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this expression in two separate ways:\n\n- The first is to find the absolute value of the product. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(-6)\\cdot 2| & =|-12| \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second is to find the product of the absolute values. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(-6)\\cdot 2| & =|-6|\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general\n\n$$\n|a \\cdot b| = |a|\\cdot |b|.\n$$\n\nThe absolute value operation is also distributive with respect to division. In general,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186056066 1064 "Let's take a number, say, $-3$. We can depict it on a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/de/ded8743284f207bff35eba24617fd4245a69d439ed151d09eed564813f9d72fb.png)\n\nThe *distance* between the number $-3$ and $0$ is known as the **absolute value** of $-3$. We write it as\n\n$$\n\\mid -3 \\mid = {\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\mid \\,2\\, \\mid$, the absolute value of the number $2$, is the distance between $2$ and $0$:\n\n![](images/f4/f44fcd05fb0932083d48c8b11892247d8de8fbc46ccf7186fb12843702181aea.png)\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\mid \\,2\\, \\mid = {\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nSo, the absolute value simply\n\n- *removes the minus sign*, if the number is negative, or\n- does nothing, if the number is non-negative.\n\nSometimes, we can be asked to evaluate an expression involving the absolute value function, like $\\mid x +1 \\mid + 5$ when $x=-3$. In this case, we evaluate the expression inside the absolute value before simplifying the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid + 5 &=  \\\\\n\\mid (-3) + 1 \\mid + 5 &=  \\\\\n\\mid - 2 \\mid + 5 &=  \\\\\n2 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186056066 1064 "Let's take a number, say, $-3.$ We can depict it on a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/de/ded8743284f207bff35eba24617fd4245a69d439ed151d09eed564813f9d72fb.png)\n\nThe *distance* between the number $-3$ and $0$ is known as the **absolute value** of $-3.$ We write it as\n\n$$\n|-3| = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $|\\,2\\,|$, the absolute value of the number $2$, is the distance between $2$ and $0$:\n\n![](images/f4/f44fcd05fb0932083d48c8b11892247d8de8fbc46ccf7186fb12843702181aea.png)\n\nWe write $|\\,2\\,| = {\\color{red}2}.$\n\nSo, the absolute value simply \n\n- *removes the minus sign*, if the number is negative, or\n\n- does nothing, if the number is non-negative.\n\nSometimes, we can be asked to evaluate an expression involving the absolute value function, like $|x +1| + 5$ when $x=-3.$ In this case, we evaluate the expression inside the absolute value before simplifying the rest of the expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1|+5 & = \\\\ |(-3)+1|+5 & = \\\\ |-2|+5 & = \\\\ 2+5 & = \\\\ 7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061690 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480774 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061690 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480774 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061691 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480811 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061691 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480811 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481046 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061692 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481046 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480863 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061693 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480863 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061696 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480991 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186061696