7592186045417 true] [17592186063984 1071 "Simplify $-3t^3\\left(3t^2+2t\\right)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063984 1071 "Simplify $-3t^3\\left(3t^2+2t\\right).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063984 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3t^{3}(3t^{2} + 2t) &=  \\\\\n(-3t^{3}) \\cdot (3t^{2}) + (-3t^{3}) \\cdot (2t) &=  \\\\\n((-3) \\cdot 3)(t^{3} \\cdot t^{2}) + ((-3) \\cdot 2)(t^{3} \\cdot t) &=  \\\\\n(-9)(t^{3 + 2}) + (-6)(t^{3 + 1}) &=  \\\\\n(-9)(t^{5}) + (-6)(t^{4}) &=  \\\\\n&= - 9t^{5} - 6t^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063984 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t) & = \\\\ (-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t) & = \\\\ ((-3)\\cdot 3)({t}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{2})+((-3)\\cdot 2)({t}^{3}\\cdot t) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{3+2})+(-6)({t}^{3+1}) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{5})+(-6)({t}^{4}) & = \\\\ -9{t}^{5}-6{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063985 1071 "Simplify $-5x\\left(x^3-4\\right)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063985 1071 "Simplify $-5x\\left(x^3-4\\right).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063985 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x(x^{3} - 4) &=  \\\\\n(-5x)(x^{3}) + (-5x)(-4) &=  \\\\\n(-5)(x \\cdot x^{3}) + ((-5) \\cdot (-4))(x) &=  \\\\\n(-5)x^{1 + 3} + (20)x &=  \\\\\n&= - 5x^{4} + 20x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063985 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x({x}^{3}-4) & = \\\\ (-5x)({x}^{3})+(-5x)(-4) & = \\\\ (-5)(x\\cdot {x}^{3})+((-5)\\cdot (-4))(x) & = \\\\ (-5){x}^{1+3}+(20)x & = \\\\ -5{x}^{4}+20x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063986 1071 "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063986 1071 "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063986 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x^{2}(\\frac{x^{3}}{2} + 3x^{2}) &=  \\\\\n(-6x^{2})(\\frac{x^{3}}{2}) + (-6x^{2})(3x^{2}) &=  \\\\\n(-6x^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}x^{3}) + (-6x^{2})(3x^{2}) &=  \\\\\n((-6) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})(x^{3} \\cdot x^{2}) + ((-6) \\cdot 3)(x^{2} \\cdot x^{2}) &=  \\\\\n(-3)(x^{3 + 2}) + (-18)(x^{2 + 2}) &=  \\\\\n&= - 3x^{5} - 18x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063986 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) & = \\\\ -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063987 1071 "Simplify $xy\\left(3x^2+xy+4\\right)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063987 1071 "Simplify $xy\\left(3x^2+xy+4\\right).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063987 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxy(3x^{2} + xy + 4) &=  \\\\\n(xy) \\cdot (3x^{2}) + (xy) \\cdot (xy) + (xy) \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n3(x \\cdot x^{2})(y) + (x \\cdot x)(y \\cdot y) + 4(xy) &=  \\\\\n3(x^{3})(y) + (x^{2})(y^{2}) + 4xy &=  \\\\\n&= 3x^{3}y + x^{2}y^{2} + 4xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063987 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy(3{x}^{2}+xy+4) & = \\\\ (xy)\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+(xy)\\cdot (xy)+(xy)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 3(x\\cdot {x}^{2})(y)+(x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y)+4(xy) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3})(y)+({x}^{2})({y}^{2})+4xy & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063988 1071 "Expand and simplify $(y + 5)(y - 2)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063988 1071 "Expand and simplify $(y + 5)(y - 2).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063988 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (y + 5)(y - 2) \\\\ &= y(y-2) + 5(y-2) \\\\ &= y^2-2y +5y-10 \\\\ &= y^2 + 3y - 10 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063988 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(y + 5)(y - 2) &= \\\n\t\ty(y-2) + 5(y-2) &=\\\n\t\ty^2-2y +5y-10 &=\\\n\t\ty^2 + 3y - 10\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063989 1071 "Expand and simplify $(2n - 1)(3n - 6)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063989 1071 "Expand and simplify $(2n - 1)(3n - 6).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063989 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (2n - 1)(3n - 6) \\\\ &= 2n(3n - 6) + (-1)(3n - 6) \\\\ &= 6n^2 - 12n - 3n + 6 \\\\ &= 6n^2 - 15n + 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063989 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2n - 1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t2n(3n - 6) + (-1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 12n - 3n + 6 &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 15n + 6\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063990 1071 "Expand $(p^2-q^2)(2p+3q)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063990 1071 "Expand $(p^2-q^2)(2p+3q).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186063990 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p^{2} - q^{2})(2p + 3q) &=  \\\\\np^{2}(2p + 3q) + (-q^{2})(2p + 3q) &=  \\\\\n&= 2p^{3} + 3p^{2}q - 2pq^{2} - 3q^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186063990 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}(2p+3q)+(-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ 2{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}q-2p{q}^{2}-3{q}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481351 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481428 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481342 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481346 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481368 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481401 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481370 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481382 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481344 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481390 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481381 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064439 1050 540.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064457 1050 540.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064596 1071 "$(7u^{2} - 6v)(v - u) =$ {{field-1}} $u^{3} +$\n{{field-2}} $u^{2}v +$\n{{field-3}} $uv -$\n{{field-4}} $v^{2}$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186064596 1071 "$(7{u}^{2}-6v)(v-u)=${{field-1}}${u}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${u}^{2}v\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$uv\\,-$\n{{field-4}}${v}^{2}$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064596 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7{u}^{2}-6v)(v-u) & = \\\\ 7{u}^{2}(v-u)+(-6v)(v-u) & = \\\\ 7{u}^{2}v-7{u}^{3}-6{v}^{2}+6uv & = \\\\ -7{u}^{3}+7{u}^{2}v+6uv-6{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064599 1071 "$2b^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4} + b^{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) =$ {{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}} $b^{3}$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186064599 1071 "$2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${b}^{3}$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064599 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ 2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{4})+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+(-\\frac{2}{3}){b}^{3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} & = \\\\ 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064676 1071 "$(q+6)(q+2)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064676 1071 "What is $(q + 6)(q + 2)$?" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186064676 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q+6)(q+2) & = \\\\ q(q+2)+6(q+2) & = \\\\ {q}^{2}+2q+6q+12 & = \\\\ {q}^{2}+8q+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064784 1071 "$-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5})=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186064784 1071 "What is $-p^{4}(-7p^{5}q^{2} + 3p^{2}q^{5})$?" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186064784 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (-{p}^{4})(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2})+(-{p}^{4})(3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (\\,(-1)\\cdot (-7)\\,)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{5})({q}^{2})+(\\,(-1)\\cdot 3)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{2})({q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (7)({p}^{4+5})({q}^{2})+(-3)({p}^{4+2})({q}^{5}) & = \\\\ 7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186065439 1064 "To expand an expression like\n\n$$\n2x^2 (4x+3)\n$$\n\nwe can use the distributive law. We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2}(4x + 3) &=  \\\\\n2x^{2} \\cdot (4x) + 2x^{2} \\cdot (3) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 4)(x^{2} \\cdot x) + (2 \\cdot 3)x^{2} &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot x^{2 + 1} + 6 \\cdot x^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 8x^{3} + 6x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186065439 1064 "To expand an expression like   \n\n![](images/cd/cd1928f00c75236e8749153ea82e10efd3fc88d435eb095a940215337086e8a9.svg)  \n\nwe can use the distributive law. We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:  \n\n![](images/22/225564303e86ce4bce242c66c78434d94114de43c144933d6cadda2b718cfbc9.svg)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186065440 1064 "When we want to multiply two binomials, we take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x + 2)$, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x + 2)$, and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x+2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} ({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})(x + 2) \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}{x}}(x + 2) + {\\color{red}{3}}(x + 2) \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + 2x + 3x + 6 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + 5x + 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186065440 1064 "When we want to multiply two binomials, we take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x + 2),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x + 2),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x+2)$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{x}}(x + 2) + {\\color{red}{3}}(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 2x + 3x + 6 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 5x + 6\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100706 1071 "Express $\\left(2\\cdot 4^3\\right)^2$ as a base raised to a single exponent." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186100706 1071 "Express $\\left(4^3\\right)^2$ as a base raised to a single exponent." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100706 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\left(4^3\\right)^2=4^{3\\cdot 2}=4^{6}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100706 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(2\\cdot4^3\\right)^2=2^{2}\\cdot 4^{3\\cdot 2}=4 \\cdot 4^{6} = 4^{7}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186100707 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-4}\\right)^{2}=2^{(-4)\\cdot 2}=2^{-8}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-8}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-8} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186100707 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-4}\\right)^{2}=2^{(-4)\\cdot 2}=2^{-8}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-8},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-8} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481361 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481435 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481384 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481386 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481387 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186100960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481426 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186101076 1050 420.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186101969 1064 "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(3^5\\right)^4\n$$\n\nby applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3^{5})^{4} &=  \\\\\n3^{5} \\cdot 3^{5} \\cdot 3^{5} \\cdot 3^{5}_(⏟)_(4copies) &=  \\\\\n3^{5 + 5 + 5 + 5} &=  \\\\\n3^{5 \\cdot 4} &=  \\\\\n&= 3^{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186101969 1064 "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ {{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119294 1078 #edb/ref 17592186119295 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481345 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481419 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481376 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481356 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186119855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481379 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186120530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186120530 1071 "Let $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}$ form a basis of $Span{v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$\n2. $dim(Span{v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}) = 3$\n3. $dim(Span{v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, w}) = 4$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186120530 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=4$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186120530 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ (not $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123213 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123214 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123215 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123217 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480820 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480870 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480928 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186123212 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481004 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186123212 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123216 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186124533 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481388 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481385 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186125102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186125102 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 11 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine whether $A = PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D$, where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186125102 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 11 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186125102 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 11 & 8-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )+20) & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128870 1071 "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128870 1071 "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128870 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n10 \\div 5^{0} + (9 - 5) = 10 \\div 5^{0} + 4\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $5^0=1$. So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\div 5^{0} + 4 &=  \\\\\n10 \\div 1 + 4 &=  \\\\\n10 + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128870 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+(9-5)=10÷{5}^{0}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $5^0=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+4 & = \\\\ 10÷1+4 & = \\\\ 10+4 & = \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128871 1071 "What is the value of  $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128871 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}$?" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128871 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that\n\n$$\n(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot (-2)^{-2} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 4} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128871 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128872 1071 "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186128872 1071 "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left(1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128872 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 + (-4)^{-2} \\div (1.5 + \\frac{2}{15})^{0} &=  \\\\\n2 + \\frac{1}{16} \\div 1 &=  \\\\\n2 + \\frac{1}{16} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{32}{16} + \\frac{1}{16} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186128872 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2}  = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{(-4)}^{-2}÷{(1.5+\\frac{2}{15})}^{0} & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16}÷1 & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{32}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{33}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481404 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481340 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481380 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481395 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481413 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481355 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481375 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481353 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481373 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186129335 1050 540.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186130481 1064 "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{0}+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+4÷2 & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186130481 1064 "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n$(-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{0} + 2^{2} \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n1 + 2^{2} \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n1 + 4 \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n1 + 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186133602 1071 "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](images/23/23aedd2796ca55056409a6d0c32e0d5521283297398bd78d8c7ac3ce84c42fba.svg)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186133602 1071 "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](images/8f/8f4bdd28c709b7f1b162e98ad196547f90c577df74e52fb6eef1d88efa209af9.png)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186133602 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/10/10e08ad8dd61b256018aa33488f94b563669fa91c739785488306d15160838c9.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/6e/6ec9d05c93c9ae7a3c3079d69b7c0b5d665fb295efaa918cea26f77988110342.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a9/a9fa0f03fc16c2d3f1c2102033c0ec6c7d4911ee2e1001a460893c85171204b8.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186133602 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/94/948d731d51fff2a1462db9eacab511eefbfdb1634bc9c45b2cf92ca67c4a4d18.png)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/e8/e8a3c7b01b547745c01f123634ca06ffc966ef9acec682af33f05066e00ceef1.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/bb/bb718fb5d9931c6f99268f40bbef401