nd{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157081 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis ![](images/48/4870d8e5469a2ccc429a0325a19a422820a65a145fa9651b55fe174b17e59841.svg) of $\\mathbb R^2,$ find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ if ![](images/45/4555a53bc8e10db083aca9cab2ffde97d414f86c5c90366da1b0ace1f82dad5d.svg)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157081 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis ![](images/5a/5a56202f818d8b8d10dd06d5e4bd02ee0aabc2f16758ca64cb6dc416fb71075c.png) of $\\mathbb R^2,$ find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ if ![](images/d9/d97e5bc94afc05532528354b82231eab87ca49ec72bd42879759c64c4165837d.png)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157081 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\, \\mathbf{q}_2.$ \n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that ![](images/1b/1ba0b151abea3c5b1a050c6ff924f3718fcb045b063b1b47df7a73f9d15316c9.png) where $x_i = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{q}_i.$ Computing the components of $\\mathbf{x},$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-2-6=-8 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-4+3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/51/51184680e9ec8b56a437334ca2a9281b1b364da2c35922839be13e545d92cec4.png)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157081 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\, \\mathbf{q}_2.$ \n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that ![](images/a7/a7be5a62fbf433bf7c0e324aeb284a9c17cf31df39c2e8cc2a9bc1ace486dc98.svg) where $x_i = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{q}_i.$ Computing the components of $\\mathbf{x},$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-2-6=-8 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-4+3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/da/da4ec39d0dbf73a9ec10828d14a2c2f3b0b2907a48e878f97b732d35b02d8200.svg)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481391 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481422 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481414 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157482 1050 420.0 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157541 1071 "Find $f^{″}(1, 0)$ for the function $f(x, y) = xy - x^{2}y^{3}$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157541 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157541 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-2x{y}^{3} & x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x{y}^{3})=-2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x{y}^{3})=1-6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{y}^{3} & 1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 1-6x{y}^{2} & -6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,0).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157768 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157768 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157769 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157769 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186157771 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186157771 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186158470 1064 "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186158470 1064 "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n![](images/82/82577033f8175a603c2f8b3b47bf5b9a9d568edf03801d58f844ca0007120f64.svg)\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n![](images/c7/c7ef03afd5772c8446f0864cc470d7375b5ddb30a97a0e19c48cec9c0dc755a4.svg)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186158471 1064 "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n*Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$* \n\n*Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then* \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186158471 1064 "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n> $$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186159668 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159672 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186159680 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159684 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186161690 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2,1\\right).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186161690 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F\\left(2,1\\right)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186161690 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i)\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2, 1) &= P(X \\le 2, Y \\le 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to\n\n$$\nX \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1\n$$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2, 1) &= ∑_(x \\le 2)∑_(y \\le 1)f(x, y) \\\\\n&= f(1,-2) + f(1,-1) + f(1, 0) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.05 \\\\\n&= 0.45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186161690 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-2)+f(1,-1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.3+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186161691 1071 "Suppose ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2