(2,4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186162413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186162413 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(10, 9) = 0.72, F(10, 6) = 0.3, F(8, 9) = 0.15, F(8, 6) = 0.08$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}, S_{Y} = {3, 6, 9, 12}$.\n\nFind $P(8 < X \\le 10, 6 < Y \\le 9)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186162413 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.72,\\,F(10,6)=0.3,\\,F(8,9)=0.15,\\,F(8,6)=0.08,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(8<X\\le 10,6<Y\\le 9).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186162413 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/04/045903aeef84fbd38d09e97f203d9169f0c1f10b662e343b82fc8113dbd1ecc3.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/81/8107e3dbe0cd180f8ce4ac8760f3fb341cb6d4accf1c698856aec361d4db23b7.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,6)$ and $(8,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,6).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8<X\\le 10,6<Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,6)-F(8,9)+F(8,6) \\\\  & =0.72-0.3-0.15+0.08 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186163341 1064 "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X$, and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i)\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y$, on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are called the **supports** of $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0, 1 \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{0, 1, 2 \\}\n$$\n\nrespectively.\n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1)$. By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(1, 1) &= P(X \\le 1, Y \\le 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to\n\n$$\nX \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1\n$$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(1, 1) &= f(0, 0) + f(0, 1) + f(1, 0) + f(1, 1) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186163341 1064 "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X,$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P( X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)  = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are the **supports** of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are $S_X = \\{ 0, 1 \\}$ and $S_Y = \\{ 0, 1, 2 \\},$ respectively. \n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1).$ By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.3+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186163360 1064 "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nas a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i)\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1)\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0$.![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1$.![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty)$.\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F(\\infty, y)$.\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y)$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186163360 1064 "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\n \n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1).\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1.$\n\n![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty).$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F( \\infty, y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y).$" 13194139535218 17592