] [17592186167135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186167135 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 14xy^{2}$ on the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{3}$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186167135 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=14x{y}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186167135 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}{∬}_{D}14x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}14x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}[7{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}7{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[{y}^{7}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186167137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186167137 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y \\ge 0$." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186167137 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186167137 1074 "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186168149 1064 "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n*Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186168149 1064 "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n> *Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n> $$\n\nFor example, let's find the average value of the function \n\n\\[\nf(x,y) =x+3y^2+2\n\\]\n\nover the region \n\n\\[\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n\\] \n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = 28, \\qquad \\textrm{Area}(D) = 4.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the average value of the function over [math]D[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf_{\\textrm{ave}}  \t\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 28 \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  7. \n\\end{align*}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186168150 1064 "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\n \n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n*If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186168150 1064 "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n> *If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n> $$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186168151 1064 "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186168151 1064 "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186172099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481405 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186172309 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186172309 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197913 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197914 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197915 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197916 13194139535218 17592186045417 false] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480883 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480887 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480936 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186197912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481051 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186197912 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480883 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186198015 1074 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7adae8ec845db2e091e9813a338b05895ebca800371df4841d327739863b863.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operato