ansformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186199451 1064 "Suppose that\n\n$$\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula: $$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.