1079 #uuid "fa568649-82c3-5879-8a09-d6b3fd2afc1c" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480761 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480814 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480847 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480969 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481232 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1077 "selection" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480847 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481339 1079 #uuid "fedc1fab-9a1c-5cb6-864b-93ad4589f535" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481243 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1069 "q-178582" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1071 "${8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4=${{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1072 #uuid "00632209-b439-5ff0-a973-a59862d161d8" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481340 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${8}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+8\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+(8\\times 4) & = \\\\ 1+32 & = \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481312 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1069 "q-146843" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,6)=0.7,\\,F(9,6)=0.5,\\,F(6,6)=0.4,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>6,Y\\le 8).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1072 #uuid "01cad121-bd37-5572-936b-c4e7143fa3ed" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481341 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/00/0036b6bd64900e2c6a0319f16788b2d563a8415256e2f21ba561b1fb41afdf34.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ and top-left corner at $(6,6),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/43/4394f404e54b8e585becb601f688eece3d4769efe2a124cb53b11d3432a9996b.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/5e/5ed86031d5762e3a2fe440fba52775fc6833243664fa18ee85c3449b50ff6cee.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6,Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.7-0.4 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481278 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1069 "q-215318" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1071 "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$-{x}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-5x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1072 #uuid "02b8d95c-cd10-56fd-a2ee-19a46309a688" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481342 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-5x) & = \\\\ (-{x}^{3})(2{x}^{2})+(-{x}^{3})(-5x) & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 2)({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-1)\\cdot (-5))({x}^{3}\\cdot x) & = \\\\ (-2){x}^{3+2}+(5){x}^{3+1} & = \\\\ -2{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481248 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1069 "q-160937" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1072 #uuid "03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481343 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481314 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1069 "q-216721" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1071 "Expand the following product of binomials. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$(11u-6)(7u+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1072 #uuid "04f1ace1-c4c7-5004-a410-92cc0ba374dc" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481344 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(11u-6)(7u+2) & = \\\\ 11u(7u+2)+(-6)(7u+2) & = \\\\ 77{u}^{2}+22u-42u-12 & = \\\\ 77{u}^{2}-20u-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481330 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1069 "q-162396" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1071 "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j$ at $t=0.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1072 #uuid "08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481345 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 2-(2t-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(8{t}^{2}-4t+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{(8(0)^{2}-4(0)+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0-0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481316 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1069 "q-216998" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1071 "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$-{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}-x+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1072 #uuid "0b6d8c42-1d59-52b0-9958-cceeca4a3dbe" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481346 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}-x+2) & = \\\\ (-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{4})+(-{x}^{2})(-x)+(-{x}^{2})2 & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4})+((-1)\\cdot (-1))({x}^{2}\\cdot x)+((-1)\\cdot 2)({x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3){x}^{2+4}+(1){x}^{2+1}+(-2){x}^{2} & = \\\\ -3{x}^{6}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481323 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1069 "q-215788" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1071 "If $x=-2,$ then $|{x}^{3}-x|+2x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1072 #uuid "0c594a87-b025-5472-b8ba-c9c1f57faafe" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481347 1074 "We substitute $x=-2$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}-x|+2x & = \\\\ |(-2)^{3}-(-2)|+2(-2) & = \\\\ |-8+2|-4 & = \\\\ |-6|-4 & = \\\\ 6-4 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481245 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1069 "q-163234" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=6xy$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1072 #uuid "0fd9a9b1-ea5c-5d82-ac2e-6cadd4a02789" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481348 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}{∬}_{D}6xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{y}^{2}}6xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[2{y}^{6}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481242 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1069 "q-146615" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,1)=0.77,\\,F(4,-1)=0.15,\\,F(1,1)=0.19,\\,F(1,-1)=0.09,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1072 #uuid "1374041d-2f69-55e3-9b42-ce863bb1bab0" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481349 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/01/01e04dfdac5f4abc16bd856dcfbe1b7361010cce2e7779658a33f293c1c52ae4.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2d2525118ca3b62c0829c0308fab168d0b806dfa13302c007a63cd354314d63f.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(1,1)+F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.77-0.15-0.19+0.09 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481303 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1069 "q-140524" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1072 #uuid "17e0153f-17ba-57ee-a920-3117ebdc5b12" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481350 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481269 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1069 "q-216986" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1071 "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$2{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}+4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1072 #uuid "1c4a8c11-58ca-529c-a377-f348add911e4" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481351 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}+4) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{4})+2{x}^{2}\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 6{x}^{2+4}+8{x}^{2} & = \\\\ 6{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481239 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1069 "q-69229" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1071 "${6}^{3/7}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1072 #uuid "1f30e377-2581-5fcb-9c99-e0f9d4fbdadd" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481352 1074 "First, using the power law for exponents, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n{6}^{3/7}={({6}^{3})}^{1/7}.\n$$\n\nThe fractional exponent ${x}^{1/n}$ can be written as the radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$\n\nComparing ${x}^{1/n}$ with our expression above, we get $x={6}^{3}$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{1/7}=\\sqrt[7]{{6}^{3}}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481313 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1069 "q-179401" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1071 "$(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1072 #uuid "2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481353 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that ${2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1+16}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{16}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481271 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1069 "q-78841" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1071 "$|-\\frac{2p}{r}|=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1072 #uuid "307f44ca-a3a8-5008-a66b-8f406f11b7bd" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481354 1074 "Since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-\\frac{2p}{r}|=|\\frac{2p}{r}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481301 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1069 "q-100293" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1071 "${7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3})=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1072 #uuid "372dbe69-5a97-5673-bcbc-f7d304887030" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481355 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6+1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 7}{7\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{7}}{3\\times \\cancel{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481283 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1069 "q-110978" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ k+30\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1072 #uuid "379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481356 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{2}=7 \\\\ 0=k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481254 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1069 "q-163257" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$ on the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1072 #uuid "3b9226c2-67e0-5858-9c64-ae46c4147a95" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481357 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481300 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1069 "q-215798" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1071 "If $x=-7,$ then $|x+2|+|x|=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1072 #uuid "3d01c032-f798-5a69-9d8e-9a45c684623b" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481358 1074 "We substitute $x=-7$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+2|+|x| & = \\\\ |-7+2|+|-7| & = \\\\ |-5|+|-7| & = \\\\ 5+7 & = \\\\ 12 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481241 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1069 "q-146338" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,0).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1072 #uuid "40beaebe-e39b-5263-8664-2ca0bd457e93" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481359 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 0.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 0}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,0)+f(2,0) \\\\  & =0.35+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481258 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1069 "q-41031" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1072 #uuid "41cad912-e122-5581-bcb3-a15aeb801b3b" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481360 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481261 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1069 "q-68372" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1071 "${({0.5}^{6})}^{3}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1072 #uuid "42cdb213-4669-5b7c-8eac-cf2da2872f82" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481361 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({0.5}^{6})}^{3}={0.5}^{6\\cdot 3}={0.5}^{18}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481329 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1069 "q-111983" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1071 "Simplify the expression $|-9a|.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1072 #uuid "4ad222fd-26eb-5815-83d7-f48153369720" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481362 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|9a|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|9a| & = \\\\ |9\\cdot a| & = \\\\ |9|\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 9\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 9|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481335 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1069 "q-86847" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1072 #uuid "4b9728bb-13e5-5813-917f-2ed0c37ac7bc" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481363 1074 "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=1,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481249 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1069 "q-160903" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1072 #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481364 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481250 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1069 "q-99490" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1071 "${5}^{3/4}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1072 #uuid "4e2076f0-c68c-52f4-9968-62b13912e45a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481365 1074 "First, using the power law for exponents, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n{5}^{3/4}={({5}^{3})}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nThe fractional exponent ${x}^{1/n}$ can be written as the radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$\n\nComparing ${x}^{1/n}$ with our expression above, we get $x={5}^{3}$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{({5}^{3})}^{1/4}=\\sqrt[4]{{5}^{3}}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481267 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1069 "q-111990" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1071 "$|14y|=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1072 #uuid "501f8db7-00d6-5d21-840c-caa3263e21e2" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481366 1074 "We distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|14y| & = \\\\ |14\\cdot y| & = \\\\ |14|\\cdot |y| & = \\\\ 14\\cdot |y| & = \\\\ 14|y|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481265 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1069 "q-163197" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=3.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1072 #uuid "510822f4-2240-59da-8db4-5275b43b2562" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481367 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{2}{3},3)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481277 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1069 "q-74584" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1071 "$6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1072 #uuid "526966d4-b472-58c0-be00-2ffec30b650e" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481368 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) & = \\\\ (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{x}{2})+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) & = \\\\ (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{2}x)+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot x)+(6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}))({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (3)({x}^{3+1})+(-2)\\cdot ({x}^{3+3}) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481311 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1069 "q-146409" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,y>6.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1072 #uuid "5555465c-a779-5eab-9f3a-3128b4e5cfe0" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481369 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>6,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/644db930cdb0d683a0bcae61b2596dbaeaa4c46df45d8347053c742d2ded3af9.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $1$ and $x>1,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $6$ and $y>6,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481259 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1069 "q-44739" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1071 "$-4{a}^{3}(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2}+3{a}^{2}+2)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1072 #uuid "5d5a3382-d5a0-5b4b-ac58-85cbe16d22b9" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481370 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{a}^{3}(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2}+3{a}^{2}+2) & = \\\\ (-4{a}^{3})(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2})+(-4{a}^{3})(3{a}^{2})+(-4{a}^{3})(2) & = \\\\ (-8)({a}^{3+4})({b}^{2})+(-12)({a}^{3+2})+(-8)({a}^{3}) & = \\\\ -8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}-12{a}^{5}-8{a}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481333 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1069 "q-83656" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1071 "Using the change of variables $u={e}^{x}$ and $v={e}^{y},$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 4\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1072 #uuid "5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481371 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481296 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1069 "q-146624" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,3)=0.8,\\,F(1,3)=0,\\,F(4,1)=0.3,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1072 #uuid "5e2590f0-16cb-5122-9f2a-082c6b4b3553" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481372 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/2b/2b159f4ba1873270f42b7afd2fdc4b969672c7d3cb7e694d519ab82dab33b283.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/e1/e186cf0e38e938798cdcd5bc10a5334230cc78cc77d114a14eba0c07702a1cdc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/eb/ebcfd8e0b553cab38a76eac77aab45186db92b3fe47a1fbb87a8623db4c29389.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,3)-F(4,1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481291 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1069 "q-100301" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1071 "Calculate ${(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1072 #uuid "5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481373 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}={(\\frac{2}{1})}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3} & = \\\\ 1-11\\times 8 & = \\\\ 1-88 & = \\\\ -87\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481331 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1069 "q-146416" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,2<y<3.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1072 #uuid "5f664747-7f72-51aa-bfa2-d2cff4da1dfc" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481374 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0$ and $2<y<3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/64dbc5d811486be64e3dd87b151d9ae8f2ae4b2cf36c3964d32d0439843a2bb5.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at least $0$ and $x<0,$ no possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481263 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1069 "q-175958" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1071 "$\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1072 #uuid "6433a3cf-883a-54aa-9f03-bdc753346e58" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481375 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${6}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{36}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times \\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 2}{36\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{36}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10+1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481238 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1069 "q-35293" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1072 #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481376 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481274 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1069 "q-146413" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $2<x<4,y<2.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1072 #uuid "65aabd4c-4895-5cab-b402-d575edfca45d" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481377 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}\\times \\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $2<x<4$ and $y<2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4c/4cfff97ef922ffe99f880771d525d616e6c5b601406597813d63425e5ab19345.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $2$ and $y<2,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481332 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1069 "q-163208" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3y-2x,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1072 #uuid "65bd6053-5f41-5a6f-ac31-815596c779fb" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481378 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[3xy-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(1,b)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 18 & =(3b-2\\cdot 1)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 4 & =3b-2 \\\\ 6 & =3b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=2.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481270 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1069 "q-74210" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(C).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1072 #uuid "66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481379 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ then the equation $Cx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 0=k-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481339 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1069 "q-100221" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1071 "${(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1072 #uuid "6932ab11-a677-5ac7-a52f-b39238ad7b0e" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481380 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${0.03}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷3-3 & = \\\\ 36÷3-3 & = \\\\ 12-3 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481319 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1069 "q-111350" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1071 "$(2{c}^{4}+3d)({c}^{4}+2d)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1072 #uuid "6e37bd4e-5f8a-5417-aabd-8b7d37d96ee7" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481381 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{c}^{4}+3d)({c}^{4}+2d) & = \\\\ 2{c}^{4}({c}^{4}+2d)+3d({c}^{4}+2d) & = \\\\ 2{c}^{8}+4{c}^{4}d+3{c}^{4}d+6{d}^{2} & = \\\\ 2{c}^{8}+7{c}^{4}d+6{d}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481266 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1069 "q-52151" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1071 "$(t+3)(t+4)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1072 #uuid "6f29a61b-d59f-5f7b-8b19-ba0d9192d12c" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481382 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t+3)(t+4) & = \\\\ t(t+4)+3(t+4) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}+4t+3t+12 & = \\\\ {t}^{2}+7t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481338 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1069 "q-69210" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1071 "Write ${10}^{1/4}$ using radical notation." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1072 #uuid "712d7269-b4cd-5fc9-b8d2-ae2c1a69f311" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481383 1074 "The fractional exponent ${x}^{1/n}$ can be written as the radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$\n\nComparing ${x}^{1/n}$ with ${10}^{1/4},$ we get $x=10$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{10}^{1/4}=\\sqrt[4]{10}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481255 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1069 "q-68148" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1071 "${({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1072 #uuid "747c9c25-846d-5f90-8282-9333af5562e1" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481384 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2\\cdot (-2)}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481295 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1069 "q-94610" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{a{t}^{3}+bt+1\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1072 #uuid "75f0d82a-dd66-5d66-8858-8e85c7e74ab7" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481385 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)=t+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =(t+1)+(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)=t+1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2(t+1)=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481325 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1069 "q-100392" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1071 "${({9}^{3})}^{-6}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1072 #uuid "77363ffd-e93f-5e69-9243-0a270550e6fc" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481386 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({9}^{3})}^{-6}={9}^{3\\cdot (-6)}={9}^{-18}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{-18},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(9)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{9}^{-18}={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481320 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1069 "q-141303" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1071 "${({(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-4})}^{6}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1072 #uuid "7c245f72-80de-502f-91e6-10b3d05c7ce1" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481387 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-4})}^{6}={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{(-4)\\cdot 6}={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-24}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481292 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1069 "q-84600" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1072 #uuid "7f2c9542-25be-572a-a669-8d12401722e1" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481388 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+24) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481297 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1069 "q-83653" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1072 #uuid "7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481389 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481288 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1069 "q-111345" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1071 "$(3k+7)(7k+2)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1072 #uuid "84908780-ec57-5aef-8661-1e98636ea221" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481390 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3k+7)(7k+2) & = \\\\ 3k(7k+2)+7(7k+2) & = \\\\ 21{k}^{2}+6k+49k+14 & = \\\\ 21{k}^{2}+55k+14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481290 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1069 "q-81138" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $20{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1072 #uuid "8702bab9-12cd-5dfe-a35e-1fe882de5059" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481391 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+4}{1+0+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n20{x}_{2}=20\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}=28.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481260 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1069 "q-212311" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1071 "If $y=10,$ then $|y-15|+2-y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1072 #uuid "8db98389-40f2-5730-bf5c-0c0bb991f194" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481392 1074 "We substitute $y=10$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|y-15|+2-y & = \\\\ |10-15|+2-10 & = \\\\ |-5|+2-10 & = \\\\ 5+2-10 & = \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481299 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1069 "q-146851" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,3)=0.6,\\,F(5,1)=0.23,\\,F(2,3)=0.32,\\,F(2,1)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1072 #uuid "8dece31e-c114-5d98-b9c8-904ef5ba8d5d" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481393 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2dd2929b39c9d0357516ebea9f1f31ed2b5f64c6154184ec904996c51162c144.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(5,3)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/db/db601c0b0d2931a61d2218e196ea7485fc8d258cb77c604dc44661687de43beb.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,3).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,3)-F(5,1)-F(2,3)+F(2,1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.23-0.32+0.05 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481298 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1069 "q-215801" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1071 "If $y=-2,$ then $|3-{y}^{4}|+|y|-8=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1072 #uuid "8e7406b5-c760-50f7-b793-e44ff061d556" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481394 1074 "We substitute $y=-2$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-{y}^{4}|+|y|-8 & = \\\\ |3-(-2)^{4}|+|-2|-8 & = \\\\ |3-16|+|-2|-8 & = \\\\ |-13|+|-2|-8 & = \\\\ 13+2-8 & = \\\\ 7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481273 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1069 "q-178596" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1071 "$((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7=${{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1072 #uuid "9362880e-cb69-5b9a-a326-3f6c64c489b9" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481395 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nNotice that $(-4)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (1-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷4+7 & = \\\\ -1+7 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481253 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1069 "q-146848" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.55,\\,F(10,3)=0.15,\\,F(4,9)=0.1,\\,F(4,3)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(5<X<11,4<Y<10).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1072 #uuid "9ab8b5f0-f462-5316-91ff-26b04644c742" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481396 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/f4/f43c5ae8d27872c4aebe235c507600b7bf1806e22b52dd3b3ff2261509ac9925.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ and bottom-left corner at $(4,3)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/60/60bf5a958b682d513010c3eff9a8084072be62e286c0b0dfacb09960a3283e38.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,3)$ and $(4,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<11,4<Y<10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,3)-F(4,9)+F(4,3) \\\\  & =0.55-0.15-0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481315 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1069 "q-78907" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1071 "$|-\\frac{8}{7z}|=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1072 #uuid "9b56ca18-7b32-5eb8-a22c-8e34fda01fb4" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481397 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{8}{7z}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{8}{7z}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|8|}{|7z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7\\cdot z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7|\\cdot |z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{7|z|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481310 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1069 "q-146412" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3,y<1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1072 #uuid "9db88b4b-2611-5ba3-82d8-88e768790963" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481398 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3$ and $y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/2e/2ef3347831324b55e1a8ddaff2b0eb6af7fb7fc171867906e70c4b51f0051b05.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<1,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481324 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1069 "q-163151" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1072 #uuid "a130ee36-c4fa-557a-b62c-af4b4bf44ed4" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481399 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+9x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+9\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =(2a+{1}^{2})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =12a+6 \\\\ 24 & =12a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481294 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1069 "q-146339" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(3,-1).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1072 #uuid "a1b40ecf-b8ac-57c2-a3f6-1fe3744073eb" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481400 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3,-1) & =P(X\\le 3,Y\\le -1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 3,Y\\le -1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3,-1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 3}{\\sum }_{y\\le -1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+f(2,-1)+f(3,-1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05+0.05 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481327 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1069 "q-217022" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1071 "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1072 #uuid "a358c984-11c0-53e4-9e16-174bbfcc06eb" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481401 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}a)(9{a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}a)(-15a) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9)({a}^{1}\\cdot {a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-15))(a\\cdot a) & = \\\\ (3)({a}^{1+4})+(-5)({a}^{1+1}) & = \\\\ 3{a}^{5}-5{a}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481336 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1069 "q-163231" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}-y$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1072 #uuid "a3d12aa9-7854-5ac5-a883-81ef95d3c722" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481402 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2{x}^{3}-4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\cdot {2}^{3}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481246 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1069 "q-163149" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1072 #uuid "a8b13a1a-6bdb-56d5-b06e-dfa9901c1db1" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481403 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+8\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 24 & =(2a+3\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 6 & =2a+3 \\\\ 3 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{3}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481257 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1069 "q-163171" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1072 #uuid "a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481404 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{3}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{2y}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}60{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[30{e}^{2y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =120-30 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481334 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1069 "q-127318" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\,y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1072 #uuid "a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481405 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481268 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1069 "q-146626" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,1)=0.6,\\,F(1,0)=0.5,\\,F(1,-1)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,-1<Y\\le 1).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1072 #uuid "af981bc5-9b24-55d4-b993-5b515801ef39" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481406 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/dd/dd49cc4b7cb5cc80bb325819c5c8f3c31f8bc3ed762139c2c3b9567a24db2324.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/34/344fec686907843b4b9375db138592f93be9d0232500fb35dceb90086db503cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e3a7389008d67f4096fdaa6a98c0e3e2bdeda64ec1e47b93a6d3ba66a54b18d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481282 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1069 "q-163277" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1071 "![](images/06/06a853735916ae3219eea02fa7e7e30d9e941106600cd5b642d951f553aace59.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the semicircle shown above." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1072 #uuid "b0a81f04-6f59-512d-aa20-c54e3b26a51d" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481407 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[-\\frac{1}{6}{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}[{cos}^{6}⁡(0)-{cos}^{6}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481317 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1069 "q-163256" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1072 #uuid "b2ff4fa5-a334-50b5-9869-f8e569ec5011" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481408 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481302 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1069 "q-131823" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1071 "Evaluate $|{2}^{y}|-5|{y}^{2}|$ for $y=3.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1072 #uuid "b3323b73-981f-531d-b13a-02484111e1fb" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481409 1074 "We substitute $y=3$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{2}^{y}|-5|{y}^{2}| & = \\\\ |{2}^{3}|-5|{3}^{2}| & = \\\\ |8|-5|9| & = \\\\ 8-5(9) & = \\\\ 8-45 & = \\\\ -37\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481326 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1069 "q-160938" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1072 #uuid "b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481410 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481322 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1069 "q-163240" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2x\\}$ given that the area of the region is $2.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1072 #uuid "bbfd9077-09aa-55c5-a7ec-ce238f7922af" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481411 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481287 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1069 "q-146340" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,4).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1072 #uuid "bf85ed85-426f-57b2-8e6b-2867b23718fe" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481412 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 1,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 1}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,2)+f(-1,4)+f(1,2)+f(1,4) \\\\  & =0.3+0.2+0.2+0.1 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481262 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1069 "q-175947" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1071 "$15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1072 #uuid "bfd0e220-31b0-52ce-8e2c-55afd941a790" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481413 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0} & = \\\\ 15+(-27)÷1 & = \\\\ 15+(-27) & = \\\\ 15-27 & = \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481264 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1069 "q-102684" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1072 #uuid "c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481414 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481293 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1069 "q-146389" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1072 #uuid "c34fa369-1de0-5740-997b-e34e018ecb92" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481415 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) \\\\  & =0.2+0.3+0.1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481244 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1069 "q-146414" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>4,y<0.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1072 #uuid "c5d1cc22-e243-59a9-86db-8bc6845a4180" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481416 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,4\\}\\times \\{0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>4$ and $y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ef/efa1ab8112333d8b91715568f5ed08c26734a6b55b1fe0a8f692beae3b92a52f.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $0$ and $y<0,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481321 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1069 "q-69236" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{{6}^{4}}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1072 #uuid "c6e04ff1-6e26-5bfa-a6e9-85e824fdf68a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481417 1074 "The radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can be written using a fractional exponent as ${x}^{1/n}.$\n\nComparing $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ with $\\sqrt[3]{{6}^{4}},$ we get $x={6}^{4}$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{{6}^{4}}={({6}^{4})}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{({6}^{4})}^{1/3}={6}^{4/3}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481309 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1069 "q-38309" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1072 #uuid "c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481418 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481252 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1069 "q-162426" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1071 "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ at $t=0.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1072 #uuid "cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481419 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2},\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4t(1+{t}^{2}),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =12{t}^{2}+4,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|4t(1+{t}^{2})\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (12{t}^{2}+4)|}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}+4}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{12(0)^{2}+4}{{((4(0)(1+(0)^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481240 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1069 "q-146849" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,1)=0.7,\\,F(1,-2)=0.3,\\,F(-2,1)=0.4,\\,F(-2,-2)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1072 #uuid "d10653a4-2ac5-5be4-9b7e-7dbc6ddafa84" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481420 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/ae/ae7d480e52257825e679349ce185b4aa08ed3b096fd7a780ece56c90439798fe.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(-2,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcb1b0b518485dfcd4066a0259fea4a2dc050be47e95b4de0b925d8f1c997aa.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(1,-2)$ and $(-2,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-2)-F(-2,1)+F(-2,-2) \\\\  & =0.7-0.3-0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481276 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1069 "q-163205" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=4.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1072 #uuid "d1845eab-f52b-5f23-bd27-faf40f79c71a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481421 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481308 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1069 "q-39491" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $3{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1072 #uuid "d1f6161d-c4cc-5098-b5d8-59c092ed97d0" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481422 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8+2}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})=-2.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481285 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1069 "q-160906" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1072 #uuid "d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481423 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481289 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1069 "q-82798" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1071 "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1072 #uuid "d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481424 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481328 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1069 "q-69209" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1071 "Write ${8}^{1/7}$ using radical notation." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1072 #uuid "d57989f6-d842-5cb5-a58c-440f55f9dbda" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481425 1074 "The fractional exponent ${x}^{1/n}$ can be written as the radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$\n\nComparing ${x}^{1/n}$ with ${8}^{1/7},$ we get $x=8$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{8}^{1/7}=\\sqrt[7]{8}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481284 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1069 "q-141291" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1071 "${({3}^{5})}^{-2}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1072 #uuid "d7b368d3-f70a-5d30-a896-1bd1fcce4d3a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481426 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{5})}^{-2}={3}^{5\\cdot (-2)}={3}^{-10}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481307 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1069 "q-69241" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{{9}^{4}}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1072 #uuid "dda23851-fa80-5ec4-9488-714cdb6cd2c1" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481427 1074 "The radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can be written using a fractional exponent as ${x}^{1/n}.$\n\nComparing $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ with $\\sqrt[3]{{9}^{4}},$ we get $x={9}^{4}$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{{9}^{4}}={({9}^{4})}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{({9}^{4})}^{1/3}={9}^{4/3}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481256 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1069 "q-44642" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1071 "$-2{w}^{3}({w}^{3}+3w+2)=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1072 #uuid "dfaf6e99-1d8f-5b5e-92bd-9fd3c571d232" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481428 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{w}^{3}({w}^{3}+3w+2) & = \\\\ (-2{w}^{3})({w}^{3})+(-2{w}^{3})(3w)+(-2{w}^{3})(2) & = \\\\ (-2)({w}^{3}\\cdot {w}^{3})+((-2)\\cdot 3)({w}^{3}\\cdot w)+((-2)\\cdot 2)({w}^{3}) & = \\\\ (-2){w}^{3+3}+(-6){w}^{3+1}+(-4){w}^{3} & = \\\\ -2{w}^{6}-6{w}^{4}-4{w}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481286 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1069 "q-82818" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1071 "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1072 #uuid "e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481429 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481251 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1069 "q-160902" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-1),$ $(2,0),$ $(3,-2),$ and $(2,-3).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1072 #uuid "e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481430 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481275 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1069 "q-160850" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1071 "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1072 #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481431 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481272 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1069 "q-160918" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1072 #uuid "ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481432 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481281 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1069 "q-86852" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1072 #uuid "ed119e3d-d405-5ea0-ae9c-40defc91b776" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481433 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46},$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{23}.\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481306 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1069 "q-160916" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1072 #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481434 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481305 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1069 "q-68146" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1071 "${((-3)^{2})}^{7}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1072 #uuid "f72e8e5a-2bd4-5b68-b3c2-b03b184545ba" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481435 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{((-3)^{2})}^{7}=(-3)^{2\\cdot 7}=(-3)^{14}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481247 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1069 "q-215791" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1071 "If $y=-3,$ then $|2y+7{|}^{6}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1072 #uuid "f8176f08-c3fb-5b9f-a7f7-c8961022e13b" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481436 1074 "We substitute $y=-3$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+7{|}^{6} & = \\\\ |2(-3)+7{|}^{6} & = \\\\ |-6+7{|}^{6} & = \\\\ |1{|}^{6} & = \\\\ {1}^{6} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481318 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1069 "q-146620" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(15,6)=0.82\\,F(12,6)=0.6\\,F(6,6)=0.12,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1072 #uuid "fab237a1-fe76-5f6d-bb1c-9924690227b6" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481437 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4dbaa771e897551b72f0c12ad2ba6940dfbef450c89815dc0f8912babd6a05.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fb/fb0624aa6e88d4811e77d3c38f24d1d20d201b8e7f6cfb23a3278afef794ab5d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.6-0.12 \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481280 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1069 "q-146845" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,1)=0.67,\\,F(3,1)=0.43\\,F(1,1)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>3,Y<2).$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1072 #uuid "fd922824-164f-5af2-954c-b939958ff429" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481438 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb5c8084958ba3dc1175c1e92035d89fb4edcdf8ce333962b49f22b40552f71.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ and top-left corner at $(3,1),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/1c/1c28f3e2f820511a30aa64ccd3affacaa6d72e69da9c596827429b244bf1129a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/ae/ae993ae6e8c0d71a42ca0e11ed5b30a8d762b3fab12771247eca262f0131c4f5.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3,Y<2) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,1)-F(3,1) \\\\  & =0.67-0.43 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481337 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1069 "q-69218" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{5}=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1072 #uuid "fe9954e2-d8b5-5670-b2ae-c502beed2e2e" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481439 1074 "The radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can be written using a fractional exponent as ${x}^{1/n}.$\n\nComparing $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ with $\\sqrt[3]{5},$ we get $x=5,$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5}={5}^{1/3}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481279 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1069 "q-82911" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1071 "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x-y\\le 4\\}.$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1072 #uuid "fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481440 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481304 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1069 "q-78933" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1071 "$|-\\frac{5p}{2}|=$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1072 #uuid "ff72e312-d04f-5312-b5f1-cf59d82ad7d7" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481441 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{5p}{2}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{5p}{2}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|5p|}{|2|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5\\cdot p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5|\\cdot |p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5|p|}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481442 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481463 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481442 1062 #uuid "098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481442 1063 #edb/ref 17592186237927 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481443 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481446 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481443 1062 #uuid "0fecd705-060a-546c-8151-ee407ac03416" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481443 1063 #edb/ref 17592186323890 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481444 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481473 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481444 1062 #uuid "1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481444 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172309 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481445 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481475 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481445 1062 #uuid "18815fe1-7a23-50e9-b4e6-4a08c8787198" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481445 1063 #edb/ref 17592186323698 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481446 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481448 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481446 1062 #uuid "1b8367ed-930a-55dd-ab5d-7c8f0aba21b9" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481446 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125102 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481447 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481462 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481447 1062 #uuid "24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481447 1063 #edb/ref 17592186481404 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [17592186481448 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481471 13194139535218 17592186045417 true] [1759218